# 1. In:
# Two inputs from the user: full_name (text string) and mobile_number (converted to an integer/number).

# 2. Process:
# Asks the user for both inputs sequentially. Converts mobile_number to an integer and adds 1 to test numeric addition as requested by the exercise instructions.

# 3. Out:
# A combined sentence printed to the console displaying both the name and the updated phone number.

# 4. My two fields, and what I would do with them:
# Full Name & Mobile Number. In a real company (e.g., CRM or customer support platform), I would use these to look up customer records or send SMS order confirmations.

# Observation Comments for "CHECK IT YOURSELF":
# - Empty line: Raises a ValueError when attempting int(""), as Python cannot convert an empty string to an integer.
# - Space: Raises a ValueError when attempting int(" "), as whitespace cannot be converted to an integer.
# - Text: Raises a ValueError when typing letters (e.g., "hello"), as string characters cannot be parsed as a base-10 integer.


# Your code below

full_name = input("Enter your full name: ")
mobile_number = int(input("Enter your mobile number: "))

# Testing adding 1 as requested by the prompt
updated_number = mobile_number + 1

print(f"Customer {full_name} has been updated in the system. Verification code sent to {updated_number}.")